## Common Anti-Patterns to Avoid

### **❌ DON'T Use Browser Dialog Functions**

```typescript
// ❌ WRONG - Browser dialogs
alert('Success!');
const confirmed = confirm('Are you sure?');
const input = prompt('Enter name:');

// ✅ CORRECT - Feedback service
this.feedbackService.showSuccessToast({
  summary: 'Success',
  detail: 'Success!'
});
this.feedbackService.showConfirmDialog(
  {
    summary: 'Confirmation',
    detail: 'Are you sure?'
  },
  () => {
    // Handle confirmation
  }
);
// For input, use proper form components with p-dialog
```

### **❌ DON'T Use Console for User Feedback**

```typescript
// ❌ WRONG - Console messages for user feedback
console.log('Item saved successfully');
console.error('Failed to save item');

// ✅ CORRECT - Proper user feedback
this.feedbackService.showSuccessToast({
  summary: 'Success',
  detail: 'Item saved successfully'
});
this.feedbackService.showErrorToast({
  summary: 'Error',
  detail: 'Failed to save item'
});
// Console should only be used for debugging
```

### **❌ DON'T Handle HTTP Errors Locally**

**MANDATORY**: Use the global HTTP error handler instead of local error handling in components.

```typescript
// ❌ WRONG - Local HTTP error handling
this.organizationService.inviteContact(requestJson).subscribe({
  next: (data: any) => {
    this.feedbackService.showSuccessToast({
      summary: 'Success',
      detail: 'Invitation sent successfully!'
    });
    this.loadData();
    this.showDialog = false;
  },
  error: (error: any) => {
    console.error('Error sending invitation:', error);
    this.feedbackService.showErrorToast({
      summary: 'Error',
      detail: 'Failed to send invitation. Please try again.'
    });
  }
});

// ✅ CORRECT - Let global error handler manage HTTP errors
this.organizationService.inviteContact(requestJson).subscribe({
  next: (data: any) => {
    this.feedbackService.showSuccessToast({
      summary: 'Success',
      detail: 'Invitation sent successfully!'
    });
    this.loadData();
    this.showDialog = false;
  }
  // No error handler - global HTTP interceptor handles all HTTP errors
});
```

**Why Use Global Error Handler:**
- ✅ **Consistent Error Handling**: All HTTP errors handled uniformly across the application
- ✅ **Centralized Error Logic**: Authentication errors, network issues, server errors handled in one place
- ✅ **Cleaner Components**: Components focus on success flows, not error management
- ✅ **Better UX**: Standardized error messages and retry mechanisms
- ✅ **Easier Maintenance**: Error handling changes made in one location
- ✅ **Automatic Logging**: Global handler can log errors for monitoring and debugging

**Exception**: Only handle HTTP errors locally when you need specific business logic that differs from the global handler (e.g., custom validation error display).


